ple, it can participate in scientific research, support complex software development, and even autonomously learn from context to complete various tasks with economic value.\nL35: Seed2.0 prioritizes user experience under large-scale online deployment, as evidenced by strong results on the LMSYS Chatbot Arena, a public human preference benchmark. Seed ranks 6th on the LMSYS Chatbot Arena — Text Arena (Overall) leaderboard and 3rd on the Vision Arena leaderboard as of Feb 16, 2026.\nL116: cite22†Image†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L117: \nL118: Join ByteDance Seed\nL119: \nL120: Models\nL121: \nL122: cite23†Seed2.1 L123: \nL124: cite24†Seedance 2.5 L125: \nL126: cite25†Seedream 5.0 Pro L127: \nL128: cite26†SeedRealtime L129: \nL130: cite27†Seed Audio 1.0 L131: \nL132: cite28†Seed GR-RL L133: \nL134: Learn More\nL135: \nL136: cite29†Publications L137: \nL138: cite30†Seed Edge L139: \nL140: cite31†Seed STEM Fellows Program L141: \nL142: cite32†Seed Campus Recruitment L143: \nL144: cite33†Transparency L145: \nL146: Copyright© 2026 Bytedance Seed\nL147: \nL148: cite34†Disclaimer Contact us : seed.feedback@bytedance.com\nL149: \nL150: Models\nL151: \nL152: cite23†Seed2.1 cite24†Seedance 2.5 cite25†Seedream 5.0 Pro cite26†SeedRealtime cite27†Seed Audio 1.0 cite28†Seed GR-RL L153: Learn More\nL154: \nL155: cite29†Publications cite30†Seed Edge cite31†Seed STEM Fellows Program cite32†Seed Campus Recruitment L156: \nL157: cite33†Transparency L158: \nL159: cite21†Image: ByteDance Seed†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L160: \nL161: Advancing the frontier of intelligence, in service of humanity\nL162: \nL163: cite22†Image†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L164: \nL165: Join ByteDance Seed\nL166: \nL167: Copyright© 2026 Bytedance Seed\nL168: \nL169: cite34†Disclaimer L170: \nL171: Contact us : seed.feedback@bytedance.com\nL172: \nL173: cite21†Image: ByteDance Seed†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com L174: \nL175: Advancing the frontier of intelligence, in service of humanity\nL176: cite22†Image†lf-flow-web-cdn.doubao.com Contact us : seed.feedback@bytedance.com\nL177: \nL178: Copyright© 2026 Bytedance Seedcite34†Disclaimer --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nopen-terminal/CHANGELOG.md at main · open-webui/open-terminal · GitHub (https://github.com/open-webui/open-terminal/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)\nciteturn31839view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31837view0\",\"pattern\":\"0.2.3\"}); Total lines: 699\nL602: ### Changed\nL603: \nL604:   * 📦 Merged output stream — PTY output is logged as type \"output\" (merged stdout/stderr) instead of separate streams, matching real terminal behavior.\nL605: \nL606: ## [0.2.9] - 2026-02-25\nL607: \nL608: ### Added\nL609: \nL610:   * 📺 Display file endpoint (GET /files/display) — a signaling endpoint that lets AI agents request a file be shown to the user. The consuming client is responsible for handling the response and presenting the file in its own UI.\nL611: ### Changed\nL612: \nL613:   * ⏳ Improved wait behavior — wait=0 on the status endpoint now correctly triggers a wait instead of being treated as falsy, so commands that finish quickly return immediately rather than requiring a non-zero wait value.\nL614: \nL615: ## [0.2.8] - 2026-02-25\nL616: \nL617: ### Added\nL618: \nL619:   * 📄 PDF text extraction in read_file — PDF files are now automatically converted to text using pypdf and returned in the standard text-file JSON format, making them readable by LLMs. Supports start_line/end_line range selection.\nL620: ## [0.2.7] - 2026-02-25\nL621: ### Added\nL622:   * 👁️ File view endpoint (GET /files/view) for serving raw binary content of any file type with the correct Content-Type. Designed for UI previewing (PDFs, images, etc.) without the MIME restrictions of read_file.\nL623:   * 📂 --cwd CLI option for both run and mcp commands to set the server's working directory on startup.\nL624:   * 📍 Working directory endpoints — GET /files/cwd and POST /files/cwd to query and change the current working directory at runtime.\nL625:   * 📁 mkdir endpoint (POST /files/mkdir) to create directories with automatic parent directory creation.\nL626:   * 🗑️ delete endpoint (DELETE /files/delete) to remove files and directories.\nL627: ### Changed\nL628: \nL629:   * 📄 Binary-aware read_file returns raw binary responses for supported file types (images, etc.) and rejects unsupported binary files with a descriptive error. Configurable via OPEN_TERMINAL_BINARY_MIME_PREFIXES env var.\nL630: \nL631: ## [0.2.6] - 2026-02-24\nL632: ### Added\nL633: \nL634:   * 🔍 File Search Endpoints: Added a new /files/glob endpoint (alias glob_search) to search for files by name/pattern using wildcards.\nL635:   * 🔄 Alias Update: Renamed and aliased the existing /files/search endpoint to /files/grep (alias grep_search) to establish a clear distinction between content-level search (grep) and filename-level search (glob).\nL636: \nL637: ## [0.2.5] - 2026-02-23\nL638: ### Fixed\nL639:   * 🛡️ Graceful permission error handling across all file endpoints (write_file, replace_file_content, upload_file). PermissionError and other OSError exceptions now return HTTP 400 with a descriptive message instead of crashing with HTTP 500.\nL640:   * 🐳 Docker volume permissions via entrypoint.sh that automatically fixes /home/user ownership on startup when a host volume is mounted with mismatched permissions.\nL641:   * 🔧 Background process resilience — _log_process no longer crashes if the log directory is unwritable; commands still execute and complete normally.\nL642: ## [0.2.4] - 2026-02-19\nL643: \nL644: ### Changed\nL645: \nL646:   * ⚡ Fully async I/O across all file and upload endpoints. Replaced blocking os.* and open() calls with aiofiles and aiofiles.os so the event loop is never blocked by filesystem operations. search_files and list_files inner loops use asyncio.to_thread for os.walk/os.listdir workloads.\nL647: \nL648: ## [0.2.3] - 2026-02-15\nL649: ### Added\nL650: \nL651:   * 🤖 Optional MCP server mode via open-terminal mcp, exposing all endpoints as MCP tools for LLM agent integration. Supports stdio and streamable-http transports. Install with pip install open-terminal[mcp].\nL652: \nL653: ## [0.2.2] - 2026-02-15\nL654: \nL655: ### Fixed\nL656: \nL657:   * 🛡️ Null query parameter tolerance via HTTP middleware that strips query parameters with the literal value \"null\". Prevents 422 errors when clients serialize null into query strings (e.g. ?wait=null) instead of omitting the parameter.\nL658: ## [0.2.1] - 2026-02-14\nL659: ### Added\nL660:   * 📁 File-backed process output persisted to JSONL log files under 'logs/processes/', configurable via 'OPEN_TERMINAL_LOG_DIR'. Full audit trail survives process cleanup and server restarts.\nL661:   * 📍 Offset-based polling on the status endpoint with 'offset' and 'next_offset' for stateless incremental reads. Multiple clients can independently track the same process without data loss.\nL662:   * ✂️ Tail parameter on both execute and status endpoints to return only the last N output entries, keeping AI agent responses bounded.\nL663: ### Changed\nL664: \nL665:   * 🗑️ Removed in-memory output buffer in favor of reading directly from the JSONL log file as the single source of truth.\nL666:   * 📂 Organized log directory with process logs namespaced under 'logs/processes/' to accommodate future log types.\nL667: \nL668: ### Removed\nL669: \nL670:   * 🔄 Bounded output buffers and the 'OPEN_TERMINAL_MAX_OUTPUT_LINES' environment variable, no longer needed without in-memory buffering.\nL671: \nL672: ## [0.2.0] - 2026-02-14\nL673: ### Added\nL674:   * 📂 File operations for reading, writing, listing, and find-and-replace, with optional line-range selection for large files.\nL675:   * 📤 File upload by URL or multipart form data.\nL676:   * 📥 Temporary download links that work without authentication, making it easy to retrieve files from the container.\nL677:   * 🔗 Temporary upload links with a built-in drag-and-drop page for sharing with others.\nL678:   * ⌨️ Stdin input to send text to running processes, enabling interaction with REPLs and interactive commands.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMemGUI-Bench/README.md at master · lgy0404/MemGUI-Bench · GitHub (https://github.com/lgy0404/MemGUI-Bench/blob/master/README.md)\nciteturn31839view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn31837view2\",\"pattern\":\"2026-02-15\"}); Total lines: 542\nL198:   * 2026-06-16: 📣 Preview: MemGUI-Agent shows promising results on long-horizon GUI agent tasks. The cite75†leaderboard†lgy0404.github.io has been updated with evaluation results and trajectory previews. Paper is coming!\nL199:   * 2026-06-11: 🚀 Refactoring MemGUI-Bench to a cite76†MobileWorld -style runtime and trajectory viewer. We will release more frontier model evaluation results on MemGUI-Bench soon!\nL200:   * 2026-02-15: 🎉 MemGUI-Bench adopted by cite77†Mobile-Agent-v3.5 ! Congrats to the Tongyi Lab team for achieving 27.1% on Easy tasks with GUI-Owl-1.5-32B. We welcome more agents to challenge the full benchmark! 🚀\nL201:   * 2026-02-09: 🗂️ Benchmark tasks now available on HuggingFace: cite78†lgy0404/MemGUI-Bench†huggingface.co L202:   * 2026-02-09: 📄 Paper released on arXiv! Check out our paper: cite79†arXiv:2602.06075†arxiv.org L203:   * 2026-02-03: Initial release of MemGUI-Bench benchmark. Check out our cite75†website†lgy0404.github.io .\nL204: ## 🌟 Research Using MemGUI-Bench\nL205: \nL206: Research using MemGUI-Bench, ordered by first public release from newest to oldest. Pull requests are welcome.\nL207:   * `2026-08` UI-Venus-2\nL208:   * `2026-07` Qwen-UI-Agent Technical Report: Toward Next-Generation Real-World Centric Foundation GUI Agents\nL209:   * `2026-06` MemGUI-Agent: An End-to-End Long-Horizon Mobile GUI Agent with Proactive Context Management\nL210:   * `2026-05` STAMP: Training Explicit Memory for Mobile GUI Agents in Controllable and Scalable Virtual Environments\nL211:   * `2026-04` UI-Copilot: Advancing Long-Horizon GUI Automation via Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization\nL212:   * `2026-02` Mobile-Agent-v3.5: Multi-platform Fundamental GUI Agents\nL213: ## 💾 Installation\nL214: \nL215: ### System Requirements\nL216: \nL217:   * Linux host with Docker and KVM acceleration\nL218:   * Permission to run privileged Docker containers\nL219:   * Python 3.12 and `uv` on the host\nL220: \nL221: The default Docker runtime image already includes the Android SDK, ADB, emulator binaries, MemGUI-AVD snapshot, and MobileWorld-compatible MemGUI-Bench runtime. Users do not need to install Android Studio, download AVD snapshots, build a local runtime image, or configure emulator paths.\nL222: ### Quick Install\nL223: \nL224:     # Install dependencies with uv\nL225:     uv sync\nL226: \nL227:     # Create local .env from the example\nL228:     uv run mg env init\nL229: ### Environment Configuration\nL230: \nL231: `uv run mg env init` creates `.env` from `.env.example`. If you prefer to create the environment file manually:\nL232: \nL233:     cp .env.example .env\nL234: \nL235: Edit the `.env` file and configure the following parameters.\nL236: \nL237: Required for Agent Evaluation:\nL238: \nL239:   * `BASE_URL`: OpenAI-compatible base URL for the agent model\n"
